### What is the problem?

Given two strings, find the length of the longest subsequence common to both. A subsequence keeps relative order but need not be contiguous. For "ABCBDAB" and "BDCAB", the LCS is "BCAB" with length 4.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LongestCommonSubsequence {
    public static int lcs(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

The National Bank of India compares a customer's spending category sequence against a reference lifestyle pattern. The LCS length measures how closely the customer's behavior aligns with the reference, allowing skipped categories. A high LCS feeds into a lifestyle segmentation model for targeted products.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.LongestCommonSubsequence;

public class LifestyleSegmentation {
    public void compare(String customerPattern, String referencePattern) {
        int similarity = LongestCommonSubsequence.lcs(customerPattern, referencePattern);
        System.out.println("Customer: " + customerPattern);
        System.out.println("Reference: " + referencePattern);
        System.out.println("LCS similarity: " + similarity);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new LifestyleSegmentation().compare("GROCERYDININGTRAVEL", "DININGTRAVELGROCERY");
    }
}
```

```text
Customer: GROCERYDININGTRAVEL
Reference: DININGTRAVELGROCERY
LCS similarity: 12
```

### How does the recurrence work?

When the current characters of both strings match, that shared character extends the best subsequence of the shorter prefixes, so add 1 to the diagonal. When they differ, the last character of one string cannot help, so take the better of dropping it (cell above) or dropping the other (cell to the left). Unlike the substring version, a mismatch never resets; it carries the best forward.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class RecurrenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "ACE", b = "ABCDE";
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
                System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "]=" + dp[i][j]);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("LCS: " + dp[m][n]);
    }
}
```

